WEBVTT

cue0139706
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Good afternoon ladies and gentlemen and welcome to each of you and all of you to the second presentation in this year's Solomon Goldman Lecture Series.

cue0139707
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
I'm Byron Sherwin, Vice President of Sperdus College.

cue0139708
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:32.000
The Solomon Goldman Lecture Series has been endowed in perpetuity in memory of the late Rabbi Solomon Goldman of Anshanmet Synagogue here in Chicago.

cue0139709
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.000
This year we are focusing the Solomon Goldman Lecture Series on great Jewish books.

cue0139710
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Last month we heard Professor Lawrence Fine of Indiana University talk to us about the Zohar.

cue0139711
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Today we will hear our own Professor Jose Faur talk to us about Yoseph Qaro and his great masterpiece of Jewish law, the Shulḥan ʿArukh.

cue0139712
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:07.000
We also today join in the worldwide commemoration this year of the 500th anniversary of the birth of Yoseph Qaro,

cue0139713
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:17.000
and in that regard I want particularly to welcome those of you who are here who claim descent from Yoseph Qaro.

cue0139714
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:27.000
There are few people in the world as qualified to speak about Yoseph Qaro as the Ezra Senzibar professor at Spurtus College, Jose Faur.

cue0139715
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Last March, when he delivered his first lecture at the college, Dr. Faur was a stranger to our college and to our city.

cue0139716
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Now he is almost part of our institutional family, and I know that many of you here have attended his classes and public lectures during the past year.

cue0139717
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Two of Dr. Faur's most prominent areas of interest are Sepharadi Jewry and Jewish Law, and these two areas coalesce in the person of Yoseph Qaro.

cue0139718
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.000
I am therefore pleased and honored to present Yoseph Faur, who will speak about Yoseph Qaro.

cue0139719
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Thank you Dr. Sherwin. Ladies and gentlemen, we will be examining the life, works, and ideas of perhaps the most influential Jew in the last 450 years.

cue0139720
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:39.012
Joseph Qaro was born in Toledo He left Spain in 1492 when he was about five years old and he died at the age of 87 in Safed Israel

cue0139721
00:02:40.012 --> 00:02:50.012
The place of birth, Toledo, symbolizes one of the most important essential elements in the vision of Joseph Qaro.

cue0139722
00:02:50.012 --> 00:03:10.012
Toledo was a cosmopolitan city. It was the only city in Spain that embraced both the Muslim Moorish tradition on the one hand and the Catholic European ideas and ideals on the other.

cue0139723
00:03:10.012 --> 00:03:30.012
Jews in Toledo spoke both Arabic and Spanish for centuries. The school of Toledo of Jews was the translator of most of the Arabic classics that passed from the Arabic into Hebrew and from the Hebrew into Latin.

cue0139724
00:03:30.012 --> 00:03:36.012
So Arabic was a very important element in the life of Toledo.

cue0139725
00:03:36.012 --> 00:03:46.012
As a matter of fact, the name Qaro symbolizes the passing from the Arabic culture into the European culture.

cue0139726
00:03:46.012 --> 00:03:53.012
In several of his writings, Joseph Qaro points out that Israel's family name was Habib.

cue0139727
00:03:53.012 --> 00:04:01.012
And Qaro means beloved. It's just the Spanish translation of a very, very famous name, Khabib.

cue0139728
00:04:01.012 --> 00:04:13.012
Now, later on, his vision of biculturalism, Muslim Jews in Muslim lands and Jews in European lands,

cue0139729
00:04:13.012 --> 00:04:24.012
This was the vision that he grew with in Toledo, becomes one of the essential elements in his Shulḥan ʿArukh, in his code of Jewish law.

cue0139730
00:04:24.012 --> 00:04:31.012
I will just say a few details of his life. Details that are terribly important.

cue0139731
00:04:31.012 --> 00:04:41.012
He was, in a sense, besides a legalist, he was a kind of a mystic. And there is a story. The story happened to be authentic.

cue0139732
00:04:41.012 --> 00:04:51.532
It is authentic and it is so fantastic that if not because of documentation, we would put the story into the realm of fable.

cue0139733
00:04:51.532 --> 00:05:23.024
Joseph Qaro tells in one of his autobiographical books called Magit Mereshit in which he records his mystic visions How when he was in Andrianapole in Turkey and later on when he was in Saloniki also was part of the Ottoman Empire he was always always in search of independent economic means by which he could study Tora and not depend either on the salary of a community or on business

cue0139734
00:05:23.024 --> 00:05:30.024
And for years and years struggled towards that aim without any success.

cue0139735
00:05:30.024 --> 00:05:36.024
He further tells that he decided to go into business and he went into a partnership.

cue0139736
00:05:36.024 --> 00:05:46.024
But because he would not come on time, engage in the study of Talmud, the partnership was broken.

cue0139737
00:05:46.024 --> 00:05:48.024
He didn't know what to do.

cue0139738
00:05:48.024 --> 00:06:04.024
He goes into the market and a strange man, tall, with features that he never saw before, but somehow inspired him, confidence, he tells him that he has a mortar, that he wants to sell the mortar.

cue0139739
00:06:04.024 --> 00:06:14.024
Joseph Qaro didn't understand much about metals and he says, well, I buy this mortar from you, what should be the price?

cue0139740
00:06:14.024 --> 00:06:31.024
The man says, how much money are you carrying with you? And he gave him the money. He goes back to his house and he brings this mortar to his wife. It was an old mortar, nothing to do with it.

cue0139741
00:06:31.024 --> 00:06:43.024
He goes to the school and his wife tries to find somebody who understands about metals, that maybe they could give him an idea what to do with the mortar.

cue0139742
00:06:43.024 --> 00:06:57.024
The mortar turned to be a mortar made of solid gold. Eventually, he sells this mortar and with the money he establishes himself in the city of Safed.

cue0139743
00:06:57.024 --> 00:07:10.024
And he is able to publish all of his masterpieces, including the Bet Yoseph, the Shukran Aruch, all of them were published with the money of that came out, the result of this mortar.

cue0139744
00:07:10.024 --> 00:07:18.024
He further tells the following story, that the man who sold them the mortar appears to him in a dream and says,

cue0139745
00:07:18.024 --> 00:07:36.036
I am you I am your destiny I am your faith And I was just facilitating to you what you always wanted I want to tell another aspect of his life that was very important and moved him to go to Jerusalem to Israel I sorry

cue0139746
00:07:36.036 --> 00:07:46.036
And that was that a famous Jew by the name of Shilomo Molho, who happened to have been the secretary to the king of Portugal,

cue0139747
00:07:46.036 --> 00:08:04.036
After realizing his Jewishness, he circumcises himself, escapes from Lisbon, goes into Europe, starts studying and preaching qabbala, and eventually is burnt alive by the Inquisition.

cue0139748
00:08:04.036 --> 00:08:18.036
Joseph Qaro envied that man and he thought that these conversos with their own behavior of the Kiddush Hashem were doing something that in a sense was far far more important than his studying of Tora.

cue0139749
00:08:18.036 --> 00:08:29.036
And inspired by the death of Shelomo Molcho, he decides to do something for the Jewish people and he devotes himself not only to study but also to writing.

cue0139750
00:08:29.036 --> 00:08:38.036
Now we will analyze his Shulḥan ʿArukh, but before let me point out that there were other works that he did.

cue0139751
00:08:38.036 --> 00:08:51.036
Joseph Qaro is the author of an extraordinary handbook, Methodologies of the Talmud, Klaliyah Talmud,

cue0139752
00:08:51.036 --> 00:08:59.036
In which he gives very short but very profound rules concerning interpretation of the Talmud.

cue0139753
00:08:59.036 --> 00:09:01.036
This is one of the works.

cue0139754
00:09:01.036 --> 00:09:15.036
He also has another book that is a collection of very brief notes on the Pentateuch and other books of the Bible that he used for his own private homilies.

cue0139755
00:09:15.036 --> 00:09:28.036
There is a third book, which is the Keshef Mishneh for thick volumes, that is the second best interpretation to Maimonides' famous code, Mishne Tora.

cue0139756
00:09:28.036 --> 00:09:42.036
In addition, he published two volumes of Rispansah. One volume devoted entirely to Jewish family law, and that's called Shalot Uchivot Maran Bet Yoseph,

cue0139757
00:09:42.036 --> 00:09:48.916
And another volume, Avkat Rohel, dealing with variety of Jewish rituals and Jewish monetary cases.

cue0139758
00:09:50.356 --> 00:10:00.056
Furthermore, as I mentioned before, there are the notes, biographical notes, Magit Misharim, which are mystical insights.

cue0139759
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.840
Mystical visions that he entertained, part of which, and only part of which, are now published.

cue0139760
00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:14.880
His true intellectual career began with the Bet Yoseph.

cue0139761
00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:20.800
Now the Bet Yoseph is a milestone in Jewish intellectual history.

cue0139762
00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.680
Why?

cue0139763
00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.000
Formally, it is a commentary.

cue0139764
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:40.620
It is a commentary on a book written in Spain in Toledo by the famous Yaʿaqoḇ Ben ʾAsher, one of the great luminary individuals coming from Germany, settling in Toledo.

cue0139765
00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:45.960
He writes a commentary, but in truth, the B'et Yoseph is not a commentary.

cue0139766
00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:59.400
It is something which both best transcends and shows the whole Jewish legal tradition beyond and behind the Tur.

cue0139767
00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:08.080
There are some very important ideas which will determine forever the course of Jewish legal development.

cue0139768
00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:10.240
And these are the following.

cue0139769
00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:27.240
Number one, whereas in ʾAshkenaz tradition, dialectics, pilpul, the analysis of the text was the determining factor,

cue0139770
00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:37.240
Joseph Qaro believed that in law, dialectics should play a very minor role.

cue0139771
00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:49.240
That law should be decided on the basis of authority. Not dialectics, but authority.

cue0139772
00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:58.240
Now, what determines authority? And here again, there are two very important insights.

cue0139773
00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:04.240
Number one, the acceptability of the community.

cue0139774
00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:16.240
A scholar is considered authoritative not only because of his legal analysis or his legal knowledge,

cue0139775
00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:43.994
But essentially when and if and how much of the Jewish community accepts his rulings It is the acceptability of the jurist by the community and that alone that will establish the authorities and the limits of a Poseg a Jewish consult

cue0139776
00:12:43.994 --> 00:12:55.494
This is totally revolutionary because normally both in Jewish and non-Jewish tradition, a legal authority is imposed on the public.

cue0139777
00:12:55.494 --> 00:13:06.994
It is imposed either by the political system or by the religious system. But it is always an authority that is imposed on the public.

cue0139778
00:13:06.994 --> 00:13:14.994
Joseph Qaro discovered that we cannot impose a Jewish authority on the Jewish public.

cue0139779
00:13:14.994 --> 00:13:30.994
It is rather the other way around. The Jewish public will choose that posse or legal writer that they recognize as authoritative.

cue0139780
00:13:30.994 --> 00:13:47.994
In this area, Joseph Qaro establishes a new idea, the interrelationship between Jewish scholarship and the Jewish community. This is one point.

cue0139781
00:13:47.994 --> 00:14:06.994
Second point. Ideally, following Jewish judiciary tradition, no one single individual should decide on his own what the law is.

cue0139782
00:14:06.994 --> 00:14:13.994
As a matter of fact, we have the concept of a Bet Din, a tribunal.

cue0139783
00:14:13.994 --> 00:14:19.994
The minimum tribunal must constitute at least three judges.

cue0139784
00:14:19.994 --> 00:14:28.994
And this is grounded on the whole notion, the biblical idea, that no individual alone could perceive the whole truth.

cue0139785
00:14:28.994 --> 00:14:37.994
Now, we were in need therefore of at least three judges.

cue0139786
00:14:37.994 --> 00:14:41.994
Where to find these three judges?

cue0139787
00:14:41.994 --> 00:14:51.994
How can we find three judges that on the one hand will be accepted by the whole Jewish community, or the majority of the entire Jewish community.

cue0139788
00:14:51.994 --> 00:15:05.988
Furthermore that these judges will last forever because we cannot go and undergo the same process generation after generation

cue0139789
00:15:05.988 --> 00:15:21.988
And finally, and finally, that they should have the imprimatur, the seal of Talmudic authority. How to find that kind of a tribunal?

cue0139790
00:15:21.988 --> 00:15:36.988
Now, an event took place while Yoseph Qaro was in Israel, in Safed. And that event is going to be transformed by Yoseph Qaro into the Shulḥan ʿArukh.

cue0139791
00:15:36.988 --> 00:15:51.988
Let me tell you what the event was. Jews, upon leaving Spain, they were almost in a parallel situation to that of the Jews leaving Auschwitz.

cue0139792
00:15:51.988 --> 00:15:56.628
40 years ago, they were totally demoralized.

cue0139793
00:15:56.628 --> 00:16:02.068
Many of them, a majority of them, had been victims of the Inquisitions.

cue0139794
00:16:02.068 --> 00:16:09.588
Many of them had seen their brothers, sisters, fathers, mothers, burnt alive at the stake.

cue0139795
00:16:09.588 --> 00:16:18.788
Totally depossessed, humiliated, no books, no honor, no dignity.

cue0139796
00:16:18.788 --> 00:16:31.588
They were scattered throughout the four corners of the world, from Goa in the East, in Asia, to the Americas in the West.

cue0139797
00:16:31.588 --> 00:16:41.908
There was a demoralization. Many of them turned into mysticism, a mysticism that eventually would degenerate into the Shabbatai Tzvi movement.

cue0139798
00:16:41.908 --> 00:16:48.188
Others became skeptics. Others, libertines.

cue0139799
00:16:48.188 --> 00:17:01.088
Jacob Beraf, who eventually became one of the teachers of Maran Yoseph Qaro, decided that what was at need was Jewish authority.

cue0139800
00:17:01.088 --> 00:17:07.388
Somehow we have to go back and establish Jewish authority.

cue0139801
00:17:07.388 --> 00:17:17.488
What the Inquisition had done in Spain was to wound, to kill, to undermine any concept of Jewish authority.

cue0139802
00:17:17.488 --> 00:17:40.982
There was Judaism fine but there is no Jewish authority At best Judaism could be an ideal but it is not an authority because it is totally absolutely powerless Cheikov Berav studying very carefully Maimonides came to the following discovery

cue0139803
00:17:41.982 --> 00:17:51.982
According to Jewish tradition, legal tradition, if somebody would have an ordination, a smicha,

cue0139804
00:17:51.982 --> 00:18:09.422
Coming all the way back to Moses, this individual, by the fact of being a link in the chain of tradition going back all the way to Moshe Rabbenu,

cue0139805
00:18:09.422 --> 00:18:17.422
that person will have legal authority to decide what Jewish law is.

cue0139806
00:18:17.422 --> 00:18:24.422
Now, this chain of tradition has been broken already in Talmudic times.

cue0139807
00:18:24.422 --> 00:18:31.422
By studying Maimonides, Jacob Beraf discovered the following.

cue0139808
00:18:31.422 --> 00:18:39.422
That the Jewish people, the Jewish community, ʿAm Yiśraʾel,

cue0139809
00:18:39.422 --> 00:18:48.022
Have the power to bestow smicha to anyone that the community chooses.

cue0139810
00:18:48.022 --> 00:18:52.222
So that there is a political dimension to smicha.

cue0139811
00:18:52.222 --> 00:18:57.722
You could be musmach because you represent Moses,

cue0139812
00:18:57.722 --> 00:19:02.722
or you could be musmach because you represent the Jewish people.

cue0139813
00:19:02.722 --> 00:19:07.622
And thereupon, Yaʿaqoḇ Beraf came with the idea

cue0139814
00:19:07.622 --> 00:19:10.622
That we should have Musmahim in our days.

cue0139815
00:19:12.122 --> 00:19:18.622
And through an extraordinary apparatus of propaganda, changes public opinion,

cue0139816
00:19:19.622 --> 00:19:24.122
and it gets about 13 individuals,

cue0139817
00:19:26.622 --> 00:19:32.622
that they are designated by the entire Jewish community, as Musmahim,

cue0139818
00:19:34.122 --> 00:19:36.622
as legal representative of the communities.

cue0139819
00:19:37.622 --> 00:19:59.982
They began already with a tribunal. In theory, this tribunal could revise every single law, including the law legislated in the Talmud. And because this tribunal would be musmach of the same legal status as that of

cue0139820
00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:16.760
The project was opposed by one single individual, Rabbi Lviv and Habib.

cue0139821
00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:22.800
By the way, the Habib, as I said, was the Hebrew name of the Qaro.

cue0139822
00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:25.520
He opposed the project.

cue0139823
00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:35.960
Eventually, the dream of Yaʿaqoḇ Bera of establishing an autonomous Jewish Supreme Court came to naught.

cue0139824
00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:42.760
The Turkish authorities, probably alerted by the Jewish people, saw in this kind of a

cue0139825
00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:52.840
Bet Din a political dimension that was unacceptable to the Ottoman Empire.

cue0139826
00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:59.760
Yaʿaqoḇ Beraf had to escape and he went to Damascus. He died, eventually he dies in Damascus.

cue0139827
00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:10.400
Now, Yoseph Qaro has a change of the program. He changes the program, he changes the vision.

cue0139828
00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:21.000
He says we could have a Bet Din, but the Bet Din should be composed of those people who are no longer alive.

cue0139829
00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:26.360
Of dead judges. How could you have it? Very simple, he says.

cue0139830
00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:33.000
A judge is a judge by virtue of being accepted as such by the community.

cue0139831
00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:41.080
And he pointed out very clearly that the Jewish communities at the time were divided into three.

cue0139832
00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:46.040
You had in Europe the ʾAshkenaz, German communities.

cue0139833
00:21:46.040 --> 00:22:01.040
And the greatest Jewish legal mind from Germany was De Roche, Dabeno ʾAsher ʾAshkenaz, who came eventually, lived and taught and died in Toledo. De Roche.

cue0139834
00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:09.040
So let's take his book, that would be one judge, the book of De Roche.

cue0139835
00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:19.840
Second, there were the North African communities, Morocco, Algiers, Libya. And these communities

cue0139836
00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:40.114
were ruled by the famous Isaac Alfasi the author of the Reef That should be the second judge And then you had the communities in the Near East the communities from the South of Spain Andalucia who ruled themselves according to the book

cue0139837
00:22:40.114 --> 00:22:48.754
of Maimonides, the Mishne Tora. And Joseph Qaro proposes taking these three books and

cue0139838
00:22:48.754 --> 00:23:03.754
And turning these three books into a tribunal investigating every single issue. And in those issues in which these three authorities agree, the law should be like them.

cue0139839
00:23:04.754 --> 00:23:16.754
If there is a dispute, like in all legal procedures, we follow the majority. And therefore we should follow two out of three.

cue0139840
00:23:16.754 --> 00:23:31.754
Now you would say, yes, but there is a flaw in this kind of legal thinking. They were not technically musmachim. Nobody actually appointed them as the judges of Israel.

cue0139841
00:23:31.754 --> 00:23:40.754
Although historically there were judges in Israel, but the ceremony, the smicha, was not bestowed upon them.

cue0139842
00:23:40.754 --> 00:23:53.754
And there Yoseph Qaro comes with this idea and says, and I, in quality of musmach, will endorse the decision.

cue0139843
00:23:53.754 --> 00:24:07.754
You have a tribunal representing the entire Jewish community. You have a musmach writing the final legal decision.

cue0139844
00:24:07.754 --> 00:24:17.754
And this final legal decision becomes the Shulḥan ʿArukh. I will explain later on what the Shulḥan ʿArukh exactly means.

cue0139845
00:24:17.754 --> 00:24:31.754
Now, again, from the point of view of law, if an individual will follow the instructions of a judge, of a bona fide judge,

cue0139846
00:24:31.754 --> 00:24:47.754
No matter what professors of law may think, the individual who follow that legal opinion, the instructions of the judge, he is within the spirit and the letter of the law.

cue0139847
00:24:47.754 --> 00:24:54.754
And this is what Shukran Aruch is. It is a legal code.

cue0139848
00:24:54.754 --> 00:25:14.988
Now any opinion contrary to the Shukran Rukh could have intellectual merits but from the point of view of law the Shekharan Rukh is unshakable because the decision was written by Amusmach

cue0139849
00:25:14.988 --> 00:25:38.988
Since the Smicha stopped with the death of Yaʿaqoḇ Beraav and Yoseph Qaro, from the point of view of law, there is no possibility to upset, to overthrow the decision of the Shukran Aruch.

cue0139850
00:25:38.988 --> 00:25:48.988
The only possibility would be that which was proposed by the first two chief rabbis of modern Israel.

cue0139851
00:25:48.988 --> 00:25:52.988
The Sepharadi and the ʾAshkenaz Rabbi.

cue0139852
00:25:52.988 --> 00:26:00.988
And if you allow me a little bit of personal comments, in my opinion, unmatched by any subsequent rabbis.

cue0139853
00:26:00.988 --> 00:26:08.988
And there was Isaac Herzog. You are familiar probably with his son, who is the president of Israel, Chaim Herzog.

cue0139854
00:26:08.988 --> 00:26:25.988
And Raḇ Uziel. And both of them believe that was high time for the Jewish people to have to reconvene a Bet Din of Musmachim with the same spirit as Yaʿaqoḇ B'Rav,

cue0139855
00:26:25.988 --> 00:26:33.988
that will be able to revise and legally decide what Jewish law is all about.

cue0139856
00:26:33.988 --> 00:26:41.988
But until this project is done, the Shulḥan ʿArukh remains a legal code.

cue0139857
00:26:41.988 --> 00:26:48.988
Now there are some ideas about these codes which are absolutely revolutionary.

cue0139858
00:26:48.988 --> 00:26:55.988
Number one, the need for unity.

cue0139859
00:26:55.988 --> 00:27:05.988
But the unity should be not one authority imposing itself on the rest of the Jewish community,

cue0139860
00:27:05.988 --> 00:27:14.988
but as he envisioned, the three major Jewish communities compromising, because this is what the Shukran Aruch is,

cue0139861
00:27:14.988 --> 00:27:19.988
compromising for the sake of uniformity.

cue0139862
00:27:19.988 --> 00:27:35.982
In other words sometimes we will have to give up our own minhagim our own idiosyncrasies because the other segment of the Jewish community disagree with us

cue0139863
00:27:37.982 --> 00:27:46.982
Compromise, according to Yoseph Qaro, it becomes an essential element in law.

cue0139864
00:27:47.982 --> 00:27:53.982
Rather than to have a geometrical view of law, that it is either my view or nothing,

cue0139865
00:27:54.982 --> 00:28:06.982
The Jewish communities have to learn how to negotiate and compromise, and ultimately from the point of view of God and the Hebrew tradition.

cue0139866
00:28:06.982 --> 00:28:20.982
It's not so important whether your view of the law is accepted, but rather the fact that all Jewish people are united in one idea.

cue0139867
00:28:20.982 --> 00:28:24.982
Compromise becomes one of the most important elements of his law.

cue0139868
00:28:24.982 --> 00:28:36.982
Second, which is again, Jewish law has to be negotiated.

cue0139869
00:28:36.982 --> 00:28:45.982
And one of the things that he establishes in the Shulḥan ʿArukh, in the introduction to the Bet Yoseph, is like this.

cue0139870
00:28:45.982 --> 00:29:04.982
A community has Bidhagim, has customs, and those customs are to be respected up to the point that they will conflict with the welfare of the rest of the Jewish communities.

cue0139871
00:29:04.982 --> 00:29:21.982
In other words, rather than just insulate ourselves and think that only we are the ones who count, we must change and try to see the legal perspective from a total national view.

cue0139872
00:29:21.982 --> 00:29:26.982
What were the implications of the Shukran al-Rukh? What happened with the Shukran al-Rukh? And what did it do?

cue0139873
00:29:26.982 --> 00:29:32.982
First of all, it resulted into an economic boom. That is true.

cue0139874
00:29:32.982 --> 00:29:38.222
The Shulḥan ʿArukh was one of the great economic tools of the Jewish people.

cue0139875
00:29:38.222 --> 00:29:40.222
And I'll explain to you why.

cue0139876
00:29:40.222 --> 00:29:51.142
Before the Shulḥan ʿArukh, international commerce, whether you're Jewish or non-Jewish, was impossible.

cue0139877
00:29:51.142 --> 00:29:59.982
Why? Because if you have a merchandise that you are exporting to a different nation,

cue0139878
00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:12.000
And then some conflicts will arise. There was no tribunal on earth that could adjudicate between two autonomous nations.

cue0139879
00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:17.000
Let me give you a simple example out of literally thousands.

cue0139880
00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:38.000
A boat belonging to a Jewish group in Venice was insured, maritime insurance, a very important Jewish business, it was insured by a Saloniki family.

cue0139881
00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:47.000
A Jewish family in Saloniki, modern Greece, part of the Ottoman Empire at the time, insured the boat.

cue0139882
00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:55.000
The boat was in The Hague, Netherlands.

cue0139883
00:30:55.000 --> 00:31:04.000
While in the harbor there is a fire breaking from the harbor and reaching from the harbor it reaches the boat.

cue0139884
00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:16.000
The boat is lost while the boat was at the harbor. Now the owners of the ship, of the boat, they claim they wanted the insurance.

cue0139885
00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:27.000
The insurance family, they say, well, the fire came not from the boat, but from the harbor. Yes? Alright?

cue0139886
00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:39.000
Now, it involved three different countries. The Ottoman Empire, Saloniki, Venice, and the Netherlands.

cue0139887
00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:46.000
There was no tribunal on earth that could adjudicate this matter.

cue0139888
00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:56.000
Because we had the Shulḥan ʿArukh, and because we had a code of law that was respected by every single Jewish community

cue0139889
00:31:56.000 --> 00:32:05.520
Throughout the globe, all kind of business transactions were in point of fact executed.

cue0139890
00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:12.320
The Shukran Aruch became the first international code of commerce.

cue0139891
00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:20.120
Because of this, Jewish scholarship flourished. Why?

cue0139892
00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:40.012
There is an aspect that no historian was able to unravel And this was that the great supporters of the Jewish scholarship at the Ottoman Empire and throughout the Sepharadi world were businessmen

cue0139893
00:32:40.012 --> 00:32:47.012
Now, why businessmen were so much interested in sponsoring Jewish scholarship?

cue0139894
00:32:47.012 --> 00:32:59.012
It was business. And the reason is because through Jewish scholarship, international law among Jews was possible.

cue0139895
00:32:59.012 --> 00:33:11.012
The Jewish school became a school to train the future lawyers. Contracts were written by these individuals.

cue0139896
00:33:11.012 --> 00:33:18.012
Cases were defended. We have literally thousands and thousands of cases coming out from the Jewish schools.

cue0139897
00:33:18.012 --> 00:33:27.012
The Jewish schools produce the future lawyers that will make international commerce possible.

cue0139898
00:33:27.012 --> 00:33:38.012
So, the Shulḥan ʿArukh was able, in a sense, to enroll the business community behind it.

cue0139899
00:33:38.012 --> 00:33:42.452
There was another thing revolutionized through the Shukran Aruch.

cue0139900
00:33:43.332 --> 00:33:51.852
Since it became a code of pragmatic importance, namely, you could do business through this code.

cue0139901
00:33:52.932 --> 00:33:58.892
This code was not only respected throughout the whole entire world, Sepharadim and ʾAshkenaz,

cue0139902
00:33:59.612 --> 00:34:05.132
but in a sense became the basis by which all the Jewish communities became united.

cue0139903
00:34:05.132 --> 00:34:17.632
And there never was so much exchange, both in populations and ideas, between Sepharadim and ʾAshkenaz as through the Shukhan Aruch.

cue0139904
00:34:17.632 --> 00:34:27.632
The Shukhan Aruch became the basis of all possible relationship between different communities.

cue0139905
00:34:27.632 --> 00:34:42.632
Last but not least, traditionally, Talmudic studies were projected into the notion of dialectics.

cue0139906
00:34:42.632 --> 00:35:15.824
You have the Pilpul Pilpul are fine sophisticated analysis of words terms in the Talmud What Yoseph Qaro made very clear is that this kind of analysis is a sterile That the Talmud is basically a book that ought to be translated into legal terminology into legal ideas

cue0139907
00:35:15.824 --> 00:35:24.024
The power of the Shulḥan ʿArukh is that the Talmud is a kind of a raw material.

cue0139908
00:35:24.024 --> 00:35:38.024
And unless we know how to formulate it into legal categories, it is not a useful type of enterprise.

cue0139909
00:35:38.024 --> 00:35:53.024
Once in this auditorium, a few months ago, I mentioned a story written by the Joseph Qaro himself.

cue0139910
00:35:53.024 --> 00:36:06.024
And at the autobiographical note about himself, in which he has a vision of an angel that comes and teaches him Tora.

cue0139911
00:36:08.024 --> 00:36:11.024
And there is a kind of discussions with this angel.

cue0139912
00:36:12.024 --> 00:36:22.024
Now, Yoseph Qaro was a very serious student of the Mishna, the first Jewish legal code in history,

cue0139913
00:36:22.024 --> 00:36:24.024
Written by Rabbeinu ha-Qadosh.

cue0139914
00:36:25.024 --> 00:36:32.024
There was a passage in that book that he was unable to unravel.

cue0139915
00:36:32.024 --> 00:36:34.024
Very difficult passage.

cue0139916
00:36:34.024 --> 00:36:40.024
As a matter of fact, he asked consults with all his colleagues.

cue0139917
00:36:40.024 --> 00:36:45.024
Nobody is able to explain the meaning of that passage.

cue0139918
00:36:46.024 --> 00:36:49.024
He asked the angel,

cue0139919
00:36:49.024 --> 00:37:02.024
He says, what this passage means? And then he says, oh, nobody was able to understand this passage since it was given at Sinai.

cue0139920
00:37:02.024 --> 00:37:11.024
And I'm going to reveal the meaning of that passage to you and you alone. You're going to be the first one who knows the meaning of this passage.

cue0139921
00:37:11.024 --> 00:37:17.024
He gives them the meaning of the passage, explains to him the passage of the Mishna, and Yoseph Qaro is so excited

cue0139922
00:37:17.024 --> 00:37:23.864
That he wants to go to the Bet midrash, to the school, and share it with his colleagues.

cue0139923
00:37:23.864 --> 00:37:41.036
As he entering into the Bet midrash he sees a little boy eight 9 years old studying Mishna And he happened to be studying that particular passage So Yoseph Qaro says to this child

cue0139924
00:37:41.036 --> 00:37:50.036
what does it mean? And lo and behold, this child knows exactly what it means.

cue0139925
00:37:50.036 --> 00:37:58.036
Now he's puzzled because the angel had told him that nobody knew it except for him.

cue0139926
00:37:58.036 --> 00:38:09.036
So he goes back to his house, prays, the angel comes and says, there's something wrong with your claims.

cue0139927
00:38:09.036 --> 00:38:16.036
You told me that I'm the first one to know this passage and I discovered there's a little boy, he knows it.

cue0139928
00:38:16.036 --> 00:38:26.036
He says, no, you were the first one. But after you brought this knowledge from heaven down to earth, it becomes accessible to everyone.

cue0139929
00:38:26.036 --> 00:38:41.036
One of the great things that Joseph Qaro made was to bring down Jewish law from the intricacies and the labyrinths

cue0139930
00:38:41.036 --> 00:38:47.436
of populistic discussions into a Shulḥan ʿArukh.

cue0139931
00:38:47.436 --> 00:38:51.836
Shulḥan ʿArukh means a well-prepared table.

cue0139932
00:38:51.836 --> 00:38:54.036
Everything is ready.

cue0139933
00:38:54.036 --> 00:38:57.236
It is up to you to stretch your hand,

cue0139934
00:38:57.236 --> 00:39:01.436
to take it, and to take advantage of it.

cue0139935
00:39:01.436 --> 00:39:06.836
In a sense, Shulḥan ʿArukh meant the liberation,

cue0139936
00:39:06.836 --> 00:39:14.076
Freedom of the Jew from the pitfalls of a clergy.

cue0139937
00:39:15.876 --> 00:39:22.076
Before the Shulḥan ʿArukh, Jewish law was the exclusive privilege of a clergy.

cue0139938
00:39:23.076 --> 00:39:27.716
A clergy that the common lay person was unable to challenge.

cue0139939
00:39:29.396 --> 00:39:35.396
Law could be so complex that an individual could be arbitrary,

cue0139940
00:39:35.396 --> 00:39:39.896
The site in one way one day, in the other way the other.

cue0139941
00:39:40.896 --> 00:39:48.296
There was a grip that the clergy had, Jewish clergy, over the Jewish people.

cue0139942
00:39:50.096 --> 00:40:00.056
We do know that one of the crucial reasons why some Jews prefer conversion to Judaism

cue0139943
00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:09.000
Because many Jews felt that the oppression of the clergy was unbearable.

cue0139944
00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:22.000
In a sense, in a sense, by formulating Jewish law in a simple way,

cue0139945
00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:46.000
And by formulating Jewish law in a way that is not only simple, but is legally meaningful, binding, the Shulḥan ʿArukh became the book by which the lay person could declare his independence from the clergy.

cue0139946
00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:50.000
Let me summarize.

cue0139947
00:40:50.000 --> 00:41:05.000
The Shulḥan ʿArukh is the dream of a little boy leaving Toledo and seeing the world, the Jewish world, coming into ashes.

cue0139948
00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:12.000
Toledo was a cosmopolitan city. Different cultures together.

cue0139949
00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:21.000
Arabic speaking Jews, Jews from France, Jews from Germany, Spanish speaking, they are all together.

cue0139950
00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:25.000
It was turned into ashes.

cue0139951
00:41:25.000 --> 00:41:41.000
As this boy goes through the Ottoman Empire, he registers the bitter divisions plaguing the Jewish world.

cue0139952
00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:50.300
He says in the time of Bechamai and Bet Hillel there were two Torahs and the rabbis complained.

cue0139953
00:41:50.300 --> 00:42:01.760
In our days we have numberless Torahs. Everybody wants to expose his views and declare all

cue0139954
00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:10.760
other Jews second rate. There was no respect for authority because the Jew did not respect

cue0139955
00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:45.994
Respect his own self What to do In a sense he created a new concept of authority If we cannot be physically geographically politically together at least we could gather the Jewish minds of the past And all of us through the respect to our past find unity and authority for our present

cue0139956
00:42:45.994 --> 00:42:56.994
I always saw what happened to the Jew in Spain as a parallel to what happened to the Jews in modern times with the Holocaust.

cue0139957
00:42:56.994 --> 00:43:05.994
In both situations we saw our dreams turn into ashes.

cue0139958
00:43:05.994 --> 00:43:14.994
In both situations the Jew was bitter against himself and against another Jews.

cue0139959
00:43:14.994 --> 00:43:20.674
In both situations there was a need for authority.

cue0139960
00:43:20.674 --> 00:43:25.754
Joseph Qaro and his generation founded the Shuhana Ruch.

cue0139961
00:43:25.754 --> 00:43:29.754
Our generation founded the State of Israel.

cue0139962
00:43:29.754 --> 00:43:37.754
In both cases we are searching for the rebirth of Jewish authority.

cue0139963
00:43:37.754 --> 00:43:54.754
In both cases, we look into the past and it's the lay person, the lay man, the lay women, that by consenting in that authority, is given it its semikha.

cue0139964
00:43:54.754 --> 00:44:00.754
The parallel thing is very important and it's very deep. Thank you very much for your attention.

cue0139965
00:44:07.754 --> 00:44:21.514
I'd like to thank Dr. Faur for this marvelous presentation.

cue0139966
00:44:21.514 --> 00:44:30.974
The two things that are always amazing are he never uses notes.

cue0139967
00:44:30.974 --> 00:44:33.694
There were no notes up here.

cue0139968
00:44:33.694 --> 00:44:37.114
And the second thing is that when he came in this morning, he said to me, how long should

cue0139969
00:44:37.114 --> 00:44:42.114
I said, about 45 minutes if you look at your watch. It's exactly 45 minutes.

cue0139970
00:44:42.114 --> 00:44:47.114
So I wonder how many of you could do that.

cue0139971
00:44:47.114 --> 00:44:52.114
Now while he's catching his breath before the question and answer period, a number of announcements.

cue0139972
00:44:52.114 --> 00:45:01.988
First of all we have prepared a reading list for you You can get them at the door if you haven already of works from our ʾAsher Library by Professor Faur as well as works on Josephine The next lecture will be on December 4th Sunday December 4th when we will be presenting

cue0139973
00:45:24.988 --> 00:45:36.988
And we will be celebrating another birthday commemoration, the 700th anniversary of the birth of Rabbi Llevi Ben Gershon Gersonides.

cue0139974
00:45:36.988 --> 00:45:44.988
And the speaker at that time will be Professor Norbert Samuelson of Temple University, who by the way is a native Chicagoan.

cue0139975
00:45:44.988 --> 00:45:53.988
The lecture after that, February 12th, will be by our visiting professor here at Spertus College for Winter 1989.

cue0139976
00:45:53.988 --> 00:45:59.988
Professor Leonard Fine, founder of Moment Magazine.

cue0139977
00:45:59.988 --> 00:46:08.988
He will be speaking on Herzl's books, The Jewish State and the Old New Land, and their relationship to the State of Israel today.

cue0139978
00:46:08.988 --> 00:46:18.988
On March 12th, exactly a month later, our visiting professor for the spring quarter, who many of you have heard a few weeks ago,

cue0139979
00:46:18.988 --> 00:46:25.988
Sages, Raḇ. Howard Schwartz, will be speaking about the tales of R. Naḥman of Bratislav.

cue0139980
00:46:29.368 --> 00:46:36.368
Our very exciting courses given by our very fine faculty, including Professor Faor, Professor

cue0139981
00:46:37.768 --> 00:46:43.268
Fine, myself and others, and also outside the door, if you don't believe we have a very

cue0139982
00:46:43.268 --> 00:46:55.268
In the very fine faculty, you'll find a copy of the October 11th U.S. News and World Report, which rated the Spertus College faculty one of the top 20 in the United States.

cue0139983
00:47:01.268 --> 00:47:08.268
Now Professor Faor will be happy to entertain questions. If you have a question, please just step to the microphone in the middle of the room.

cue0139984
00:47:08.268 --> 00:47:13.488
We have been taught the famous expression,

cue0139985
00:47:13.488 --> 00:47:15.488
Diya dem hachus ha-dina.

cue0139986
00:47:15.488 --> 00:47:17.488
The law of the land prevails.

cue0139987
00:47:17.488 --> 00:47:22.768
Those who live outside Israel must abide by the law of the land in which they live.

cue0139988
00:47:22.768 --> 00:47:35.982
Under what conditions are we permitted not to abide by the law of the land Under what conditions may we be allowed not to observe strictly what the Shulḥan ʿArukh dictates

cue0139989
00:47:35.982 --> 00:47:50.982
First of all, a very important notion. The Jew, before Napoleon, considered himself as a nation, as an autonomous nation in exile.

cue0139990
00:47:50.982 --> 00:48:00.982
As a matter of fact, when Napoleon offers citizenship to the Sepharadi Jews in Amsterdam, the Sepharadi Jews refuse to accept citizenship.

cue0139991
00:48:00.982 --> 00:48:14.982
Because citizenship will mean, therefore, that you have to abide by the law, you have to dismantle your Jewish courts, and in point of fact, and this is a very interesting, delicate issue, renounce to the land of Israel.

cue0139992
00:48:14.982 --> 00:48:19.982
Because if you are a citizen of a country, you cannot say, I am in Gaul, and I want to have another country.

cue0139993
00:48:19.982 --> 00:48:28.222
So, for a while, the whole notion of citizenship and so on, it was a very, very important issue.

cue0139994
00:48:28.222 --> 00:48:38.622
But, to be brief and direct to your question, at the time of the Shulḥan ʿArukh, the Jews conceived themselves as a nation in exile.

cue0139995
00:48:38.622 --> 00:48:49.102
So much so, that when the first Jews arrived to America, and they had this, you know, with the governor of New Amsterdam, Stuyvesant,

cue0139996
00:48:49.102 --> 00:49:00.502
One of the reasons why they were not so much welcome in America, because explicitly they said that we should be recognized as members of the Jewish nation in exile.

cue0139997
00:49:00.502 --> 00:49:07.102
Therefore, for they didn't serve in the army, they didn't pay taxes individually. They paid taxes only as a body.

cue0139998
00:49:07.102 --> 00:49:10.102
That is so far as history is concerned.

cue0139999
00:49:10.102 --> 00:49:27.102
From the point of view of Judaism today, we do observe all the laws of the Malchuta as long as they don't interfere with either family law or ritual.

cue0140000
00:49:27.102 --> 00:49:35.102
And thank God that we happen to be living in America in which there is separation of church and state,

cue0140001
00:49:35.102 --> 00:49:40.182
And here we could be a full American citizen and a full Jew.

cue0140002
00:49:40.182 --> 00:49:46.582
So in this country we have absolutely no problem because there is a separation of church and state.

cue0140003
00:49:46.582 --> 00:49:59.982
And the Jewish law say very clearly, you obey all the laws of the state as long as they don't interfere with the basic Jewish law.

cue0140004
00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:02.240
Let me give you an example, a very important example.

cue0140005
00:50:03.680 --> 00:50:07.160
According to Protestant ethics,

cue0140006
00:50:09.560 --> 00:50:12.160
let's see, Luther, yes,

cue0140007
00:50:13.160 --> 00:50:19.040
an individual has to obey the state even though the state will tell him to do something against his conscience.

cue0140008
00:50:19.880 --> 00:50:23.800
Which therefore means that somebody like Eichmann,

cue0140009
00:50:23.800 --> 00:50:31.400
According to that system of thinking, he is excused because he has to follow what the state tells him.

cue0140010
00:50:31.400 --> 00:50:37.160
According to Judaism, we obey the state up to a point that the state will tell us.

cue0140011
00:50:37.160 --> 00:50:44.840
If the state would tell me, go and work in a concentration camp, and from 9 to 5, and kill little children, men, women, and this,

cue0140012
00:50:44.840 --> 00:50:47.640
we say no to the state. That is part of Jewish tradition.

cue0140013
00:50:47.640 --> 00:51:04.640
But, so therefore Jewish tradition is very careful to distinguish between those laws that do not contravene basic scriptural commitment and other, the commercial, financial laws of the state that we have to obey in full.

cue0140014
00:51:04.640 --> 00:51:16.640
Professor Faur, you indicated that Joseph Qaro made a Shulḥan ʿArukh that would apply to the entire Jewish people.

cue0140015
00:51:16.640 --> 00:51:21.640
Would you comment on the Mapa of Isserles?

cue0140016
00:51:21.640 --> 00:51:26.640
Very important question that you're raising and that's one of the reasons I didn't want to elaborate

cue0140017
00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:30.640
because I knew that the question is going to be brought into the subject.

cue0140018
00:51:30.640 --> 00:51:38.640
There is of course the famous Rabbi Moshe Isserlin from Poland, Krakow,

cue0140019
00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:47.140
who wrote notes in which many, many points disagrees with Joseph Qaro.

cue0140020
00:51:47.140 --> 00:51:50.340
Two very important observations.

cue0140021
00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:59.640
Number one, there was a very lively and friendly correspondence between both Joseph Qaro and Raḇ Moshe.

cue0140022
00:51:59.640 --> 00:52:05.940
Very, they have the chuvot. Beautiful, very nice comradeship and respect.

cue0140023
00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:24.940
Second, the very fact, not only the Rama, but the countless ʾAshkenaz commentaries, write note, or notes rather, to the Shulḥan ʿArukh, only strengthen the Shulḥan ʿArukh.

cue0140024
00:52:24.940 --> 00:52:40.294
All what the Rama says says that in our particular city or place we have a different custom In other words this is the law Shukran Aruch is the law We have different customs

cue0140025
00:52:40.294 --> 00:52:52.294
And he points the custom and in a way makes it easier for the Shukran Aruch to acclimatize itself to Eastern European communities.

cue0140026
00:52:52.294 --> 00:52:59.294
If the Shulḥan ʿArukh would have remained without the Rama, it would have been rejected.

cue0140027
00:52:59.294 --> 00:53:07.294
Because we know how people are, people don't like to abandon family or community tradition.

cue0140028
00:53:07.294 --> 00:53:17.294
By writing those glosses, the Rama makes it possible to accept the Shulḥan ʿArukh in Eastern European communities.

cue0140029
00:53:17.294 --> 00:53:23.294
So I don't see it as anti-Shulḥan ʿArukh, but rather I see it as pro-Shulḥan ʿArukh.

cue0140030
00:53:23.294 --> 00:53:28.294
And one of the great contu... and that's the way Mappa, you know what the word Mappa means?

cue0140031
00:53:28.294 --> 00:53:34.294
It means a table cloth. Notice you have a table, and he's just doing something adding to it.

cue0140032
00:53:34.294 --> 00:53:42.294
He's making it fancier. So it's not a negation of, but a strengthen of the Shulḥan ʿArukh.

cue0140033
00:53:42.294 --> 00:53:55.534
I wish Dr. Faur could explain what the Shulchan Buar says to such a person. It seems to me difficult.

cue0140034
00:53:55.534 --> 00:54:06.174
I learned once in Haida, in Numbers, in the Tora, the Midbar Jew, the gutter sticks in Shabbat.

cue0140035
00:54:06.174 --> 00:54:10.174
There was the law of God.

cue0140036
00:54:10.174 --> 00:54:19.174
Other law in Deuteronomy, in Deuteronomy, is a law if a man get married in this guild,

cue0140037
00:54:19.174 --> 00:54:26.174
he, you know, this wife, next days, they find out she was not a virgin, should be stoned to death.

cue0140038
00:54:26.174 --> 00:54:35.174
In such a law is, but a rebellious son, a son, a murderer, not obey father and mother, the same thing.

cue0140039
00:54:35.174 --> 00:54:40.174
But I not contest the law that you should be stoned to death.

cue0140040
00:54:40.174 --> 00:54:45.174
I'm not authority, I'm a plain common Jew.

cue0140041
00:54:45.174 --> 00:54:53.174
But I wonder why should not the authorities put a special hangman?

cue0140042
00:54:53.174 --> 00:55:12.988
He should kill he should stone Why should I put my hands in blood They say the Tora says Qal b Eidu Qal a On another place say Qal b Iroh All the citizens the men from this guild from this state from this village they shall stone

cue0140043
